From 1da19f14e094874ae9e3d758c2ba99b83c2ea5ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:12:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: CI-run progress follow-ups from PR #2040
 reviews (runner wording, idle/busy runners, busy cap, sweep race, stranded
 release)

Non-blocking points from the PR #2040 reviews 5889677459, 5890809735 and 5891212407
(card t_01a1d487dbf081d6):

1. Runner wording when a list is unreadable: _fetch_runners returns None when the repo
   or org actions/runners read fails and no online runner matched, so runners_readable is
   only set when both lists were read (or one matched). A repo served only by org runners,
   read with a token without org admin, showed a readable but empty repo list and the
   message said "no online runner has them" though the org list was never read; it now
   falls back to "runner list unreadable, so they may be offline or saturated". The wake
   and the park refusal are unchanged (still not_starting).
2. shared_blocker_guard / _released_as_probe: a release without after_run
   (probe_stranded_blockers, older rows) now also needs no re-assertion of the blocker
   after it. Before #2040 the guard ignored releases, so a card that re-asserted the
   blocker after the stranded release held the released card for another interval;
   honouring the release regardless allowed a second probe in one interval. The docstring
   records the difference from pre-#2040 main and the one case left (another card's probe
   run after the release that ended without settling the wall).
3+5. Busy vs idle runners: only an online runner whose busy flag is not false counts as
   saturated (a runner without the flag keeps the old reading). When every matching
   online runner is idle while the job waits 15 min (a stuck runner, or one in a runner
   group the repo cannot use) the run is not_starting and the comment names the idle
   runner; one busy runner among idle ones stays saturated (a runner between jobs is
   briefly idle). Busy holds a wake back for at most BUSY_LIMIT_SECONDS (2 h of queueing):
   past it the run is not_starting and the comment names the cap, so a false busy from an
   org runner in another group cannot keep the card asleep forever.
4. submit_sweep re-checks the running sweep and SWEEP_EVERY_SECONDS under _LOCK after
   _has_ci_wait (_sweep_due), so two racing ticks on one board start one sweep.
6. kanban_shared_blockers.task_applies is public; the guard, probe_stranded_blockers and
   the CI sweep's _others_behind use it.

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_ci_progress.py adds a test per item (org or repo list
unreadable -> wording fallback with the wake kept; idle runner -> wake; busy among idle and
no busy flag -> busy; busy under/past the cap; two racing submits -> one sweep; stranded
release + later re-assertion -> held, control without it -> released). Every added
condition has a killing mutant: 14/14 killed (sdlc-review mutants.py over
test_kanban_ci_progress.py + test_kanban_shared_blockers.py, baseline 110 passed).
Replay of the guard change over worker-7's kanban DBs (read-only): 0 release events,
1 card behind a blocker, 0 verdicts changed; the change only holds more, restoring
pre-#2040 behaviour for that case.

Rebased over caf4d5dad4 (unreadable runner list + a job running on the labels = busy): a partial read (org list
unreadable, no online repo match) now returns None from _fetch_runners, so caf4d5dad4's recent-job fallback applies
there as well as when both lists 403; new test test_org_list_unreadable_without_a_repo_match_falls_back_to_recent_jobs
(killed by reverting the None return). The recent-job fallback is not capped by BUSY_LIMIT_SECONDS: its evidence is
this repo's own jobs, so the runner-group false-busy case the cap exists for cannot arise there.
